Issues.Body:
## Summary
A cursor that reads a vector column of `mlContext.Data.Cache(view)` straight after the call, while the cache is still being filled, intermittently throws

```
System.ArgumentOutOfRangeException: If specified, must be non-negative. (Parameter 'valuesCount')
   at Microsoft.ML.Data.CacheDataView.ColumnCache.ImplVec`1.Fetch(Int32 idx, VBuffer`1& value)
```

The rows are fine — the same scan succeeds in other runs on the same data — so a row is read before it is completely written.

## Reproduction
Microsoft.ML 5.0.1, .NET 10.0.402, macOS arm64. A text file of 4,000,000 rows, `a,b,c,d,y` with four random floats and a class number:

```csharp
var ml = new MLContext(1);
var loader = ml.Data.CreateTextLoader(new TextLoader.Options
{
    HasHeader = true, Separators = [','],
    Columns = [new TextLoader.Column("Features", DataKind.Single, 0, 3), new TextLoader.Column("Label", DataKind.Single, 4)],
});
var cached = ml.Data.Cache(loader.Load(path));
var column = cached.Schema["Features"];
using var cursor = cached.GetRowCursor([column]);
var get = cursor.GetGetter<VBuffer<float>>(column);
VBuffer<float> value = default;
while (cursor.MoveNext()) get(ref value);   // intermittently throws in Fetch
```

Each run builds a fresh `MLContext`, loader and cache. Measured: more than a quarter of 20 runs threw. With an in-memory source (`LoadFromEnumerable`, 3,000,000 rows) the rate is lower (about 1 run in 30), so it depends on the reader having to wait for the filler.

## What avoids it
A cursor over all columns that reads no value, run to its end before the real scan (`using var c = cached.GetRowCursor(cached.Schema); while (c.MoveNext()) { }`), gave 0 failures in 20 runs on the text file. A cursor over no columns did not help (7 in 80 failures on the enumerable source), and neither did a cursor reading only the scalar label.

## Notes
Not verified on x64: the failure may depend on the weaker memory ordering of arm64 (the reader seeing the row count before the row's offsets). A consumer that trains on tables of millions of rows meets it as a training run dying at the start of its search about half the time.

Workaround in the consumer: cache through a helper that fills the copy (the all-column no-read pass above) before returning it.
